LeetCode 160 — Intersection of Two Linked Lists
5
Problem

Given two singly linked lists, return the node where they intersect.

If they do not intersect, return:

null
Important

Intersection means the same node/reference, not merely the same value.

For example:

A:  4 → 1
          ↘
            8 → 4 → 5
          ↗
B:  5 → 6 → 1

The intersection node is the actual node containing 8.

Approach 1 — Brute Force
Idea

For every node in List A, traverse the entire List B and check whether they are the same node.

if (a == b)

Not:

if (a.val == b.val)

because two different nodes can have the same value.

Code

public class Solution {
    public ListNode getIntersectionNode(ListNode headA, ListNode headB) {

        ListNode a = headA;

        while (a != null) {

            ListNode b = headB;

            while (b != null) {

                if (a == b) {
                    return a;
                }

                b = b.next;
            }

            a = a.next;
        }

        return null;
    }
}

Dry Run

Suppose:

A: 1 → 2 → 8 → 9
B: 3 → 4 → 8 → 9

Assume 8 is the same node.

Check A = 1

Compare:

1 == 3 ❌
1 == 4 ❌
1 == 8 ❌
1 == 9 ❌
Check A = 2
2 == 3 ❌
2 == 4 ❌
2 == 8 ❌
2 == 9 ❌
Check A = 8
8 == 3 ❌
8 == 4 ❌
8 == 8 ✅

Return node 8.

Complexity

Let:

m = length of A
n = length of B
Time  = O(m × n)
Space = O(1)

This is brute force.

Approach 2 — HashSet
Idea

Store all nodes of List A in a HashSet.

Then traverse List B.

The first node already present in the set is the intersection.

Important

Store the node itself:

set.add(curr);

not:

set.add(curr.val);

because intersection is based on reference identity.

Code

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class Solution {
    public ListNode getIntersectionNode(ListNode headA, ListNode headB) {

        Set<ListNode> set = new HashSet<>();

        ListNode curr = headA;

        while (curr != null) {
            set.add(curr);
            curr = curr.next;
        }

        curr = headB;

        while (curr != null) {

            if (set.contains(curr)) {
                return curr;
            }

            curr = curr.next;
        }

        return null;
    }
}

Dry Run
A: 1 → 2 → 8 → 9
B: 3 → 4 → 8 → 9
Store A
Set = {1, 2, 8, 9}

Now traverse B.

Node 3
3 ∈ Set ? ❌
Node 4
4 ∈ Set ? ❌
Node 8
8 ∈ Set ? ✅

Return 8.

Complexity
Time  = O(m + n)
Space = O(m)

Better time, but uses extra memory.

Approach 3 — Find Lengths and Align Lists ⭐

This is the approach in your code.

Main Idea

If the lists have different lengths, their starting positions are not aligned.

Example:

A: 1 → 2 → 3 → 8 → 9
B:       4 → 5 → 8 → 9

Lengths:

A = 5
B = 4

Difference:

5 - 4 = 1

Move A forward by 1:

A:       2 → 3 → 8 → 9
B:       4 → 5 → 8 → 9

Now both pointers have the same number of nodes remaining.

Then move them together.

Your Code

Your code is essentially this:

/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * public class ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode next;
 *     ListNode(int x) {
 *         val = x;
 *         next = null;
 *     }
 * }
 */

public class Solution {
    public ListNode getIntersectionNode(ListNode headA, ListNode headB) {

        int i = 1, j = 1;

        ListNode head1 = headA;
        ListNode head2 = headB;

        while (head1 != null) {
            head1 = head1.next;
            i++;
        }

        while (head2 != null) {
            head2 = head2.next;
            j++;
        }

        while (i > j) {
            headA = headA.next;
            i--;
        }

        while (j > i) {
            headB = headB.next;
            j--;
        }

        while (headA != headB) {
            headA = headA.next;
            headB = headB.next;
        }

        return headB;
    }
}

⚠️ Understanding i = 1 and j = 1

Normally we calculate length as:

int lenA = 0;

while (headA != null) {
    lenA++;
    headA = headA.next;
}

Your code starts with:

int i = 1;

and increments after moving:

while (head1 != null) {
    head1 = head1.next;
    i++;
}

So after traversing a list of length N, i becomes:

N + 1

The extra 1 exists for both lists, so the difference:

i - j

is still exactly the length difference.

So your logic works.

However, for readability, I recommend the standard len = 0 version.

Dry Run — Length Alignment

Consider:

A: 1 → 2 → 3 → 8 → 9
B:       4 → 5 → 8 → 9

Assume:

8A == 8B

meaning they are actually the same node.

Step 1 — Calculate lengths
Length A = 5
Length B = 4

Difference:

5 - 4 = 1

Move A once:

A: 2 → 3 → 8 → 9
B: 4 → 5 → 8 → 9
Step 2 — Move together

Compare:

2 == 4 ❌

Move:

A → 3
B → 5

Compare:

3 == 5 ❌

Move:

A → 8
B → 8

Compare:

8 == 8 ✅

Return:

8

Standard Version of Approach 3

I recommend writing it this way in interviews:

public class Solution {
    public ListNode getIntersectionNode(ListNode headA, ListNode headB) {

        int lenA = 0;
        int lenB = 0;

        ListNode currA = headA;
        ListNode currB = headB;

        // Calculate length of A
        while (currA != null) {
            lenA++;
            currA = currA.next;
        }

        // Calculate length of B
        while (currB != null) {
            lenB++;
            currB = currB.next;
        }

        // Align starting points
        while (lenA > lenB) {
            headA = headA.next;
            lenA--;
        }

        while (lenB > lenA) {
            headB = headB.next;
            lenB--;
        }

        // Find intersection
        while (headA != headB) {
            headA = headA.next;
            headB = headB.next;
        }

        return headA;
    }
}

Complexity

We traverse the lists a few times:

Time  = O(m + n)
Space = O(1)

This is already optimal in time and space.

Approach 4 — Two Pointer Switching ⭐⭐

This is the most elegant solution.

Idea

Use two pointers:

pA
pB

When pA reaches the end of A:

pA = headB

When pB reaches the end of B:

pB = headA

So each pointer travels:

A + B

and:

B + A

Therefore, they travel exactly the same total distance.

Eventually they meet at the intersection.

Code
public class Solution {
    public ListNode getIntersectionNode(ListNode headA, ListNode headB) {

        ListNode pA = headA;
        ListNode pB = headB;

        while (pA != pB) {

            pA = (pA == null) ? headB : pA.next;
            pB = (pB == null) ? headA : pB.next;
        }

        return pA;
    }
}

Dry Run

Use:

A: 1 → 2 → 8 → 9
B: 3 → 4 → 5 → 8 → 9

Lengths:

A = 4
B = 5
Initially
pA = 1
pB = 3

Not equal.

Move:

pA = 2
pB = 4
Next
pA = 8
pB = 5
Next
pA = 9
pB = 8
Next

pA reaches null.

So:

pA = headB;

Now:

pA = 3
pB = 9
Next
pA = 4
pB = null

Since pB == null:

pB = headA;

So:

pA = 4
pB = 1

Continue:

pA = 5
pB = 2

Then:

pA = 8
pB = 8

Now:

pA == pB

Return 8.

Why Does Pointer Switching Work?

Suppose:

A = a + c
B = b + c

where:

a = unique part of A
b = unique part of B
c = common part

Pointer A travels:

A + B
= a + c + b + c

Pointer B travels:

B + A
= b + c + a + c

They are equal:

a + c + b + c
=
b + c + a + c

Therefore they reach the intersection at the same time.

Case: No Intersection

Consider:

A: 1 → 2 → 3
B: 4 → 5

Pointer A travels:

1 → 2 → 3 → null → 4 → 5 → null

Pointer B travels:

4 → 5 → null → 1 → 2 → 3 → null

Eventually:

pA = null
pB = null

Therefore:

pA == pB

and return:

null

So we don't need a separate "no intersection" check.

All Approaches Comparison
Approach	Idea	Time	Space
1. Brute Force	Compare every A node with every B node	O(m × n)	O(1)
2. HashSet	Store A nodes	O(m + n)	O(m)
3. Length Alignment ⭐	Find lengths and align starts	O(m + n)	O(1)
4. Two Pointer Switching ⭐⭐	Traverse A+B and B+A	O(m + n)	O(1)
Interview Recommendation
If asked for brute force:
Nested loops
If asked for better:
HashSet
If asked for O(1) extra space:
Length + Alignment
Best / Most Elegant:
Two Pointer Switching
🧠 Pattern to Remember

This is a classic two-pointer linked-list alignment problem.

The most important code to remember is:

while (pA != pB) {
    pA = (pA == null) ? headB : pA.next;
    pB = (pB == null) ? headA : pB.next;
}
Mental shortcut
Pointer A → A + B
Pointer B → B + A

Same total distance → they meet at the intersection.

Final optimal complexity: O(m + n) time and O(1) extra space.
